$DIR/main.p:16:2: cannot pass &x to yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:27), which keeps it after returning
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of seq (declared at main.p:15).
    yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:27) stores p in package
    variable keep (main.p:30), so &x would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If yield (which may call the loop body at main.p:27) should keep its own
    copy of x, change it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can
    keep: an owned[int] from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a
    vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:36:17: cannot pass the loop body to stash, which keeps it after returning
    A range loop over a function (at main.p:36) calls the function with a yield
    function whose body is the loop body, a closure built in Stashed's frame
    (section 12.11); yield may be called only while the loop runs. stash stores
    yield in package variable keepYield (main.p:21), so the loop body would
    outlive Stashed's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change stash to call yield only while it runs, without keeping it (language
    spec section 12.11).
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
